 some  numbers to it and actually crunching the  data seems like a pretty good place to  start  and so what did  the data tell you Ben does it does it  point in the direction obviously the  Chinese government must have been the  ones to orchestrate all this is that is  that fingerprint been discovered this  well that's what it appears I mean it's  hard it's hard to it's hard to say that  definitively what one of the researchers  says is that he's only you know he's  only seeing this type of behavior when  it is state backed but you know just  looking at the numbers it's they kind of  speak for themselves I mean 50 percent  of these tweets that were sent at him  had came from accounts with fewer than  13 followers like almost four thousand  tweets didn't even he almost four  thousand and the accounts didn't even  exist until he sent his first tweet I  mean so you know he was bombarded by a  hundred and seventy thousand tweets that  at the peak of this troll activity they  were appearing at his phone at a rate of  like nearly two per second which you  know you just think about like if you're  actually trying to keep up with your  Twitter replies on your phone to get a  hundred and seventy thousand I mean it's  just astonishing and and the fact that  so many of them seem to spout pro-china  rhetoric so you know an anti Hong Kong  sentiment when the organic discussion if  you take out those accounts that had  zero followers and appear to be linked  with China it's actually the opposite  there was a lot of support for Daryl  Morey and a lot of support for the Hong  Kong protest so they were trying to make  it seem like the discourse was balanced  when in fact it really was not Ben Cohen  Wall Street Journal columnist here on  the Rich Eisen show so if we are  assuming then that it was state backed  the question is is what's the chicken or  the egg here is it is it that the  Chinese government saw this or a member  of the Chinese government saw this and  then immediately 