t Journal and discussing what we  are discussing and as you say it's kind  of crazy we're talking about communist  papers and things like that on on a  sports show if you will is that this  could cost the job of an executive of  the year who puts together Hardin and  Westbrook for an organization that could  win the NBA championship right now who  just bestowed upon him because of how  terrific he is at his job  long-term contract because a tweet got  blown up by a whole bunch of bots  because he strolled into a geopolitical  story that is so monumental it involves  the President of the United States  President G LeBron James Adam silver  Nike I mean Warner Brothers movies I  mean it is insane how many parts of our  world this touch has been and don't  forget like all of this happening  against trade negotiations and a tariff  war that could disrupt the global  economy you're totally right it is it's  absurd that it has come to this but I do  think it's I mean I struggle to think of  a more consequential story for the NBA  in a very long time and in terms of like  geopolitical implications in terms of  the tension that existed here for a  while in terms of the magnitude of the  crisis I mean it's it's one of the most  consequential and also the most bizarre  episodes I think maybe even in the  history of American professional sports  I mean I don't know how you really top  this well there's a couple of other  things I want to hit with you before I  let you go from your piece again as you  point out you cover the NBA for Walt The  Wall Street Journal you're not really an  expert on again international  informational warfare but here's the  cart achill you wrote or with your  colleagues when it comes to information  warfare China tends to be defensive and  engage on issues relating to sovereignty  according to the researchers while a  country like Russia is more aggressive  and inflames online discussions of  polarizing topics such as NFL  quarterbacks kneeling during the  national anthem and you quote one of the  researchers is saying they meaning  Russia don't care about Colin Kaepernick  they just want you to talk about Colin  Kaepernick have they found that the  Kaepernick issue was inflamed by Russia  they is that been confirmed by these  folks yeah and in fact they have my  colleague Andrew Pete and wrote a great  story for the journal right around this  time last year using some of these some  of this research from these same  professors who looked at tweets that  were backed by Russia and found that  like among the many people who were  talking about Colin calf  or continue to talk about colin  kaepernick were Russian trolls on  Twitter and you know that was them going  out of their way to show discord in the  United States whereas China tends to  like China got involved in the NBA  because an NBA general manager tweeted  about Hong Kong protests and you know  that is a third rail issue as you know  most NBA fans know by now in you know  and and you know but Colin Kaepernick  has no connection to Russia right I mean  there's no reason for Moscow to care  whether or not Colin Kaepernick gets  signed or you know him million during  national anthems but they understand  that it contributes to the political  discussion in the United States and that  it is a very easy way to inflame tension  so for now China appears to only engage  on issues that matter to them whereas  Russia is more advanced and more  aggressive and just likes to you know  get in the middle of things and mix it  up and you know that person yelling at  you on Twitter about Colin Kaepernick  could easily be a Russian troll believe  it or not so what's the more what's what  happens next year with the with Maury  and the NBA based on all that you've  gleaned on what obviously the NBA will  read your article with your colleagues  and try and put it all together with a  tip-off coming next Tuesday what do you  think happens been going yeah it's  amazing that the 